Here then is its being rooted in the Ancestral Ways; here then is its being sown as the Ancient Word to sprout forth as the Ancestral Account of the Kʼicheʼ People of the Pueblo Kʼicheʼ in the Ancient Ancestral Place known as Kʼicheʼ.

Here then is the sowing of the Ancient Word; the rooting of the Ancestral Account that reveals as it tells of all that was brought to fruition in the Citadel City of Kʼicheʼ by the Kʼicheʼ People of the Pueblo Kʼicheʼ in the Noble Realm of Kʼicheʼ: pa Tinamit Kʼicheʼ Ramaqʼ Kʼicheʼ Winaq.

Here we shall bring forth its being shown-ukʼutunisaxik; its being revealed- uqʼalajobʼisaxik; and its being told-utzijoxikʼ as that which is known as the Sowing, the Dawning; Ewaxibʼal, Saqiribʼal; the Sowing of the Sacred Seed of Sustenance; the Dawning of Noble Life of the Kʼicheʼ Sun.

Here we shall bring it out as its expression; as its revelation; and its recitation of all that was hidden in the Shadows-Ewaxibʼal to be brought forth in the Light-Saqiribʼal by those who are known; by those who are named:

Tzʼaqol, Bʼitol;

Former, Shaper; Molder, Moderler; She that Forms; He that Shapes Alom, Kʼajolom; Bearer, Begetter; Mother, Father Jun Ajpu Uchʼ, Jun Ajpu Utiw;

One Blowgun Possum Grandfather; One Blowgun Coyote Grandfather Saqi Nimaʼ Aq, Saqi Nimaʼ Sis Great White Jabali Grandmother; Great White Coati Grandmother Tepew Qʼukumatz;

Sovereign Plumed Serpent Ukʼuʼx Cho, Ukʼuʼx Palo;

Heart of the Lake, Heart of the Sea AjRaxa Laq, AjRaxa Tzel;

He of the Plate of Sacrifice; He of the Bowl of Sacrifice Maker of the Green-Blue Plate; Maker of the Blue-Green Bowl Chuchaxik, Rach bʼixik, Rach tzijoxik As it is Said; as it is Named; as it is Recited by those who are: Ri Iyom, Mamom;

Mid-Wife, Matchmaker; Grandmother, Grandfather Xpiyakok, Ixmuqane; He who comes forth from the Tortoise Shell Earth;

She of the Buried Mazorca of Maize; She of the Buried Seed-Tooth of Maize Matzanel, Chʼuqenel;

Embracer, Shelterer; Enveloper, Protector Kamul Iyom, Kamul Mamom; Twice a Grandmother; Twice a Grandfather

From now on the Mother-Fathers of the Ancient Word; Chuch-Qajaw Ojer Tzij will transcribe their Divine Ancestors Ancient Word by following the Ancient Books and Painted Lienzos where Maya Glyphs and Painted Pictorial Histories will tell the Sacred History we know as the Popol Wuj; this Mythic Masterpiece of the Maize People; this Sacred Book of Council that reveals as it tells the essential nature of the Cultural and Natural World of the Maya Culture and Mesoamerican Civilization. Popol Wuj is truly a treasure trove of Ancestral Knowledge that humanity has yet to acknowledge which is why we must to what we can to have it heard as the Ancient Word; Ancestral Account of the Maya People; Maya Pueblo who lived out their lives formed and shaped by the mythic word and mythic metaphors found here in the Popol Wuj. Let us continue as the Noble Scribes ʼIntroduceʼ us to their ʼwriting by transcribingʼ their Popol Wuj.

There exist the First Books-Nabʼey Wujil with their Ancient Script-Ojer Tzibʼam yet those who are the Seers of them-Ilol re and those who are the Contemplators of them-Bʼisol re only hide themselves away.

Yet once great was the Long Performance and once great was the Long Recitation; the Long Incantation that brought forth the complete emergence of the Sky-Earth; Kaj-Ulew with its Divisions; with its Partitions invoked here as:

Its Four-fold Cornering of its Four Corners;

Its Four-Fold Siding of its Four Sides; Its Four-fold Cornering; its Four-fold Siding; Its Four-fold Corners; its Four-fold Sides.

Its being stretched-out; its being measured-out; Its Forming of Divisions; its Shaping of Partitions;

With its Four-fold staking of its Four-fold Corners; With its Four-fold staking of its Four-fold Sides.

Its being measured by halving its measuring-cord in-two;

Its being measured by doubling its measuring-cord in half.

Its measuring-cord being stretched into the Center of the Sky;

Its measuring-cord being stretched into the Center of the Earth.

Its umbilical cord being stretched-up and out into the Womb of the Sky; Its umbilical cord being stretched down into the Womb of the Earth;

It also sounds a lot like a word-play played on the Principal Bird Deity known as Seven Scarlet Macaw-Wuqubʼ Kaqix who is a Nagual of Ukʼuʼx Kaj; the Animal Transform of Heart of the Sky-Hurricane-One-Footed-One: Ukʼuʼx Kaj Jun Raqan. Wuqubʼ Kaqix or Seven Scarlet Macaw is seen throughout the night in the Dry Season when the Popol Wuj was performed. Wuqubʼ Kaqix pivots around the North Pole of the Night Sky as Ursa Major-the Big Dipper who is seen as having only ʼOne Leg-Jun Raqanʼ and it is he who must be defeated if the rain is to fall from the Night Sky by its being covered-over in the Clouds of the Rain Season. The Heart of the Sky is reprimanded here by Quetzalcoatl who begs his divine twin to give of himself by giving sacrifice while at the same time to honor the word of Quetzalcoatl and his desire to bring forth the Forming and Shaping of the Wooden People.

The significance and sentiment of this playful reprimand by Qʼuqʼkumatz is clear when he says: ʼcome-on Ukʼuʼx Kaj donʼt humility or mock the word of the mouth of the countenance of Sovereign Lord Quetzalcoatl. While at the same time saying: ʼcome-on, get with it, humble yourself in order to sacrifice yourselfʼ; ʼcome now Jun Raqan; it is up to you Hurricane to collaborate as complementary twin by supporting the divine desire and sacred intention of Tepew Qʼuqʼkumatz who wants to bring forth the Divine Powers of Creation once again through the Forming, the Shaping of the Wooden People.ʼ This is the invocation made by Qʼuqʼkumatz to Jun Raqan Kaqulja as they sacrifice themselves to the effort of forming and shaping the next attempt at creating the Providers and Sustainers there on the Face of the Earth.

So let us turn to the Forming; the Shaping of the Wooden People: Puppet People; Doll People; Decorated Decoy People known as Poy; those of Worked Wood; those of Carved Wood;

Atiʼt Mama; Chuch-Qajaw: Grandmother-Grandfather; Mother-Father

Ancestral Lineages of the Kʼicheʼ Maya Kingdom

Ajawarem Kʼicheʼ Maya; el Reino Kʼicheʼ Maya Divine Ancestors of the Maize People Saqiram Ojer Ixim Winaq Ixim Winaq Maize People The Maya Myth of the Maize People: Ixim Winaq Divine Ancestors: Grandmother, Grandfather Ancestral Couple: Xpiyakok, Ixmuqane Ancient Mother-Fathers; Primordial Pair; Divine Duo Father, Uncle: Jun Junajpu, Wuqubʼ Junajpu Complementary-Collaborative Hero Twins: Junajpu, Ixbʼalamkej Trickster Twins Devout Twins Beloved Brothers Devoted Grandsons Dutiful Sons Engendered Sons and Entitled Grandsons Divine Ancestors and Devout Descendants Xpiyakok, Ixmuqane; Jun Junajpu, Wuqubʼ Junajpu Junajpu, Ixbʼalamkej Jun Junajpu, Wuqubʼ Junajpu: First Fathers of the Maize People; Maize Lords; Maize Deities known as One and Seven Ancient Ancestor; First Ones of Tula-Tollan; Ancient Hunter of the Blowgun; First Fathers of the Blowgun Hunters; First Ones; Ancient Ones of the Sacred Place Thick with Reeds-Pu, Ancestral Fathers of the Land Thick with Abundance, Place Thick with Life living in the Reeds: known and named as Pu-Tula-Tollan; the Mythic City of Ancestral Perfection where the Arts and Sciences of the Mesoamerica Civilization reach a State of Sublime Perfection; Aesthetic Civilization; Cultural Genius in Quest of Lifeʼs Beautification,; Noble Quest for Scientific Sophistication; Calendrical and Architectural Perfection, Priestly Quest of Astronomical Precision through Continual Observation and Tabulation; Creation of Writing and Illustration; Arts and Artisans Call to Creation of Images founded in Divine Imagination; Artistic Tradition of Mesoamerican Aesthetic Civilization; Creation of Iconographic-Pictographic Illustration and Notation; Myth-Making Narratives; Mythopoetic Genius; Forming and Shaping of Lineage Keeping Storytelling; Mythical Imagination of Imaginal Realm; Knowing the Face of the Earth through Sacred Journeys and Pilgrimage; Initiation; Rites of Passage; Singing Remembrances; Sung Recollection of Our Origins;

The Messenger Owls perched themselves above the ballcourt to ʼthrow downʼ their message like a ball thrown-tzaq into play. They did this in order to ʼform-tzʼaqʼ their words just exactly as they were ʼmade-tzʼaqʼ by the Lords to be repeated precisely; repeated with precision; repeated as perfectly ordered words ʼmadeʼ to be repeated as the perfect repetition of words ʼthrown into playʼ just as they were sent to be said as the message from the Lords of Xibʼalbʼa-Ajawabʼ Xibʼalbʼa.

This was when they, the Lords, sent their message to be repeated to the ball- players who played there on their Ballcourt-Jom known as Nim Xol Kar Chaaj.

And so it was that the Owls were sent to send the message sent by those who were the Lords of Xibʼalbʼa: Jun Kame, Wuqubʼ Kame;

Ajal Puj, Ajal Qʼana;

Chʼamiya Bʼaq, Chʼamiya Jolom;

Xikiripat, Kuchumakikʼ;

Ajal Mes, Ajal Toqʼobʼ;

Xik, Patan, these are the names of all the Noble Lords-ri Ajawabʼ Xibʼalbʼa.

Itʼs time you are told: AjAwabʼ means those of ʼillness-yawabʼ-diseaseʼ which a play on the plural of name of Ajaw-Lord which is Ajawabʼ or Lords. So this is an amazing play on the name of the Lords of Xibʼalbʼa who are called Ajawabʼ Xibʼalbʼa who are Lords of Disease Ajawabʼ Yawabʼ which is really a play on the naming of the names of the Lords-Ajawabʼ of Illness-Yawabʼ-Disease.

These names and titles that are descriptive words that ensoul and embody deep cultural meaning that I prefer not to translate; especially Lords or Ajawabʼ if possible because of the ʼdouble entendreʼ that is embodied and ensouled in this name that Mother-Fathers of the Ancient Word wanted to bring to life their inscription of their Puebloʼs Popol Wuj. These Scribes made sure their ʼword-playʼ was included because this is how the original Scribes of the Ancient Word willingly wanted to ʼname the namesʼ of the Lords of Xibʼalbʼa: Ajawabʼ Xibʼalbʼa.

This is the Maize Lineage of Jun Junajpu, Wuqubʼ Junajpu whoʼs life and light knew no end for they were the First Fathers of the People Maize who knew their origin at the beginning of their existence but knew no ending that would be the end of their days for they were Maize People: Ixim Winaq who were a People of Maize that would multiply and grow as the Providers and Sustainers of their many generations of their many lineages. The eternal essence of the Maize People lives and dies to live again as the sprouting forth of the skull-seed of the Maize Deity; Maize Lord Jun Junajpu whoʼs eternal heart; eternal soul are as everlasting and eternal as an eternal kernel of maize is hard. And as you know one must be ʼhardʼ if one is to ʼsurviveʼ on the Face of the Earth.

For the eternal since of a kernel of maize is as hard as a skull; a skull-like seed is hard as can be but when buried beneath the earth it transforms from within to transform into a tender sprout that shoots up and out of the underworld of death to rise up into the warmth and light of life. This is the moment when the hard skull-like seed cracks-open with thunder and lightning to then rise-up as a sprout out with the moisture of the rain. The maize sprout shoots up from the Underworld like the spittle of Jun Junajpuʼs skull that he spits as the ʼpitzʼ in the right palm of Ixkikʼ right hand where and when the First Father of Maize engenders his Sons; his Boys; his Twins will be conceived by their Moon Mother, IxKikʼ. IxKikʼ who conceives them by taking them in like a seed sown in the earth; like a kernel of maize sown in the earth like a skull-seed;

like an eternal kernel of maize buried at death to sprout back to life in warm embrace and loving light of their Eternal Father the Sun.

Remember the First and Second Seated Ones are the Men-Made of Wood, those who are known and named as ri Poy or Ajam Cheʼ which is the first way this tricky ʼNaming of the Namesʼ throws one off. We are thrown off the count because the first one bit is the third to sit in his noble place; his sovereign position. This then is the Third Position on the Noble Bench there in the Council House of Xibʼalbʼa; Popol Ja where the Noble Lords meet to be named by the Jun Chikop the trickster messenger known as Shawn-Xaʼn who is a kind of mosquito that is not a mosquito at all but a big biting Gnat which again there to make sure the myth-teller instills confusion where order must reign. Good luck with this; good luck with that; donʼt let yourself get bit by the Xaʼn or bit by the Us or bit by the Uqʼ who are all here to suckle blood that will be the way of Lord Storm-Tojil who will suckle the hearts and blood of those who sacrificed to him to take what feeds him with sacred sustenance; the sacred sustenance of hummingbird. Nabʼe-First; Kabʼ-Second Rox-Third; Kaj-Fourth; Roʼ-Fifth; Waqaq-Sixth; Wuq- Seventh; Wajxaq-Eighth; Bʼelej-Ninth; Lajuj-Tenth; Julaj-Eleventh; Kabʼlajuj-Twelfth;

Roxlajuj-Thirteenth; Kajlajuj-Fourteenth… This is a foreshadowing of the Fourteen Generation of the Kʼicheʼ Maya Rulers that begin with the First Four Fathers of the Kʼicheʼ Maya. So do realize that these Lords of Death; Lords of Disease may very well be the Ancestors of the Kʼicheʼ Maya as seen in their Ancient Ancestral Way of understanding Death and Disease. They sow the seed of our ʼnaming the namesʼ of the Fourteen Noble Generations of the Kʼicheʼ Winaq; Kʼicheʼ Amaqʼ

Nabʼe; Kabʼ; Rox; Kaj; Roʼ; Waqaq; Wuq; Wajxaq;

Bʼelej; Lajuj; Julaj; Kabʼlajuj; Roxlajuj; Kajlajuj… First, Second, Third, Fourth, Fifth, Sixth, Seventh, Eighth, Ninth, Tenth, Eleventh, Twelfth, Thirteenth, Fourteenth…

After all, Venus can also be seen as the Morning Star placed in the ʼHouse of Dawnʼ in the eastern dawning sky at and about the December Solstice; this is when Venus sits inside of the Temple-House formed by Scorpius at the beginning of the Dry Season. This is a hierophany which one experiences as sacred for those who live their lives in the Maya-Mesoamerican latitude of 14˚41ʼʼ as we do here in the Western Highlands of Guatemala.

Ixbʼalamkej called upon the Animals; he summons the Animals: Coati, Jabali known as Coatimundi, Peccary spoken of as Sis, Aq were they who were invoked by Ixbʼalamkej. It was he who called up those known as the Small Animals, the Big Animals; Chʼuti Chikop, Nima Chikop.

In the night, it was just in the dark before the dawn that Ixbʼalamkej asked them to bring their sustenance, their food-kechaʼ: Naqi taq pa iwechaʼ chi jujunal?

“What is the food of each one of you; you, who are the Animals? This is what I ask; I ask you to bring me your sustenance,” he asked them there, the One known as Little Jaguar-Deer.

“Utz bʼa la!” they said then when they went out to bring back their sustenance; the sustenance that was theirs and theirs alone. It wasnʼt long before they returned with all that was theirs:

There were those who went out to bring back only rotten things;

There were those who went out to bring back only flat leaves of maize;

There were those who went out to bring back only rocks;

There were those who went out only to bring back earth as the food that fed them; the sustenance that sustained them is what the Animals brought back to Ixbʼalamkej there in the Night; there in the Darkness before the Dawn.

Truly varied was the diverse sort of food that was brought back by the Small Animals, Large Animals-Jalajoj kechaʼ ri Chʼuti Chikop, Nima Chikop.

“What is this that you have brought here with us?” demanded the Lords of Xibʼalbʼa. “Where is the one who brought that here?” insisted the Xibʼalbʼa. “Where is he? Where? Show me the one who brought that here?” cried Xibʼalbʼa. This then was the defeat of the Lords of Xibʼalbʼa by Junajpu, Ixbʼalamkej.

Great suffering did they endure, but the Boys did not die there despite everything that was done to them in Xibʼalbʼa.

Ixim Winaq-Maize People;

the Divine Ancestors of the Maize People IxMuqane, Xpiyakok;

Jun Junajpu, Wuqubʼ Junajpu;

Junajpu, Ixbʼalamkej The Boys just left maize planted as a sign through which their Grandmother, Ixmuqane could remember them as a sign of their living and dying; sowing and sprouting; dying and drying to be sown again in order to return to life reborn as the replacement of the original of face of the ancestral seed. Indeed, this is the story of the perpetual lifecycle of maize with its eternal return from death comes rebirth; from death comes life just as life leads to death. Death, the time and space into which the hard dry skull headed seed is sown by being buried; by being hidden beneath the earth in order to sprout forth again in an everlasting lifecycle with neither beginning nor end. Yet, a lifecycle that necessitate those who will act and speak as the Twins act and speak when they seed their ancestral seed within ground just as their mother received the spittle as the shot of pitz from their father who engendered them ʼas ifʼ he had scored a goal by sending a shot through the ballcourt marker on the right side of the court as in the right hand of IxKikʼ the Moon Maiden who hungered to ʼenter the wordʼ like Young Maidens often hunger for the fruit that they are forbidden. But now her Boys the Engendered Sons of Jun Junajpu are sowing the seed that will produce the maize from which the First Four Lord of Kʼicheʼ will be formed and shaped in the hands of IxMuqane. There is perfection in this Myth of Maize known as Popol Wuj. Now the Hero Twins must do the sacred work of sowing their people maize seed when their Grandmother defied maize by naming it with divine names.

Now they danced, they became Dancers; they made their way dancing; they made their way by dancing all their dances; dances that they now danced by their dancing as Dancers:

Just the Whip-poor-will dance they danced-xa Xajoj Pujuy;

Just the Weasel dance they danced-xa Xajoj Kux;

Just the Armadillo dance they danced-xa Ibʼoy xkixajo; Just the Centipede dance-xa Xtzul;

Just the Stilts dance they danced-xa Chʼitik xkixaj chik.

Whip-poor-Will Dance; Dance of the Whip-poor-Will; xa Xajoj Pujuy Weasel Dance, Dance of the Weasel; xa Xajoj Kux Armadillo Dance, Dance of the Armadillo; xa Xajoj Ibʼoy; Centipede Dance, Dance of the Centipede; xa Xajoj Xtzul;

Stilts Dance, Dance of the Stilts xa Xajoj Chʼitik chik

These were the Dances they danced as Dancers of the Dances:

Xa Xajoj Pujuy; Just the Dance of the Whip-poor-Will Xa Xajoj Kux; Just the Dance of the Weasel Xa Xajoj Ibʼoy; Just the Dance of the Armadillo Xa Xtzul Xajoj; Just the Dance of the Centipede Xa Chʼitik Xajoj chik; Just the Dance of the Stilts did they dance next.

Xibʼalbʼa looked on astonished as they watched astounded by all that the Boys did; the way they performed marvels; the magical way they entertained the people.

When they burnt a house down it looked just like they had burnt the house down to the ground. Then, instantly, they raised the house right back-up again so that they left the house exactly the same as it was before; before it was burnt down to the ground to be raised right back up again. Thick with on-lookers were their performances as those of Xibʼalbʼa watched everything they did intently especially when they sacrificed one another.

This was when one was killed by the other; just laid-out flat like a corpse. First one would sacrifice the other to then-in an instant-bring him back to life again.

The Messengers arrived before the Lords to receive their instruction in their words: “ʼCome so that we can see you; come so that we might watch you perform. Come, come so that we might watch you so that we too might come to see you do all that you are said to do? Come to us, come so that you might astonish and astound us so that we might also come to admire you, say the Lords,ʼ this is what you are to say to them,” were told the Messengers when the Lords told them-their Messengers-what to say and do. The Messengers arrived there with the Dancers and told them everything the Lords had said. And so it was that the Messengers said what they were sent to say.

This then is what the Meebʼa, the Poor Boys, the Orphan Boys said:

“We wouldnʼt like that; we would not like to do that. That would be true punishment for poor folk like us; that would just be pain and suffering for dancers like us. We do not enjoy doing things that make us feel shame and we will feel shame dancing for Noble Lords in the House of Noble Lords. No, that is just too shameful; something so shameful as that is not for us. Just look how filthy we are; our poor appearance; and our eyes; just look at our bulging eyes from our poverty; the poverty of orphans. Canʼt you see that we are only simple dancers?” they said to the Messengers who listened in silence knowing what these Orphan Boys would force them to do, while allowing them to say what they had to say as the Two Boys continued on with their words; and their making of words. “Besides, what would we say to all our poor friends and companions who want to see our dancing without ever having seen or watched us dance our dances to liven up their lives with a little bit of our dancing? What would we say to them;

They just thought about the Sowing when they remembered the Dawning; for truly this then was when they anxiously anticipated the Dawn as a soulful remembrance of what was to come as a heartfelt reminiscence of the Sowing; a recollection of the Dawning that awaited them there at the Coming Out Place of the Sun: Relebʼal Qʼij.

Xere kikʼuxlaʼn ri rewaxik; usaqirik Their Soulful Remembrance of the Sowing; the Dawning Truly excited were they to be so very animated because of their soulful remembrance and recollection of their duty and dedication to bring forth the Sowing of the New Era; the Dawning of the New Sun; the Creation of the New Age which was their sacred obligation-patan; which was their divine burden-patan to be borne-patan on their back to carry in the tumpline-patan slung across and over the crown of their head-kijolom-their skull; the carrying of the ancestral cargo-patan that was theirs to carry: the Sowing; the Dawning: Rewaxik; Saqarik. This was their divine burden to be borne out on their back. This is what they now remembered as they anxiously awaited the Sowing; of the Dawning. Xere kikʼuxlaʼn ri rewaxik; usaqirik.

It was to the Sowing just as it was to the Dawning that they made their humble request; just praying with great reverence did they make their affection appeal before the Dawn; for they were those who made their needs known through their sweet plea; through their powerful petition to the Kabʼawil who were gathered there before the Sowing, the Dawning-Rewaxik Saqarik.

They were those of beloved words; of the precious truth-e loqʼ tzij;

These were the loving ones; the kind ones; the sweet ones-e ajloqʼ;

It was they who were the beloved people; the adored people-e ajloqʼ; They who were the meek ones; the modest ones-e ajnim-humble ones;

They the obedient ones; the dutiful, devoted, dedicated one-e ajnim;

These were the reverent ones; respectful ones-e ajxobʼ; These were those of deep respect; people of high regard-e ajxobʼ;

For it was they who gave sacrifice to sustain and maintain the Sky-Earth; Kaj-Ulew for this indeed was who they were and who their descendants would come to be.

They raised their faces to the Heavens-chikipaqʼabaʼ kiwach chi Kaj;

They lifted their eyes to the Sky-chikipaqʼabaʼ kiwach chi Kaj; Praying for their Daughters; Pleading for their Sons;

Imploring, insisting upon the Sky; they just imposed as they impressed upon the Heart of the Sky; Heart of the Earth-Ukʼuʼx Kaj; Ukʼuʼx Ulew their needs.

With their Eyes raised to the Sky; With their Eyes raised to the Heavens With their hearts set afire and their soul filled with desire they brought forth their words that burned ablaze as if on a sacrificial pyre calling out for all that they aspired and wished to acquire for their entire lot of their descendant daughters and resplendent sons.

This then was when they began to beg and beseech; implore and entreat Ukʼuʼx Kaj; Ukʼuʼx Ulew with secret sacred speech for all that they needed making a plea as they pleaded for their wants and wishes to be conceded on behalf of their Pueblo and People for whom they would most willingly bleed.

This then was when they began their prayer; their petition for all that was required; for all that was requested and desired for their beloved Sons and Daughters on the Face of the Earth.

Thus they intone with true inspiration their incantation, invocation, and enchantment of their honored, admired, and revered Kabʼawil: “Aqaroq; Saqaroq; To the Rising Sun, To the Coming of the Dawn!

At Tzʼaqol, At Bʼitol!

You Former, You Shaper;

You Molder, You Modeler;

You Crafter, You Creator; You Plotter, You Planner!

You Builder, You Maker! AjTzʼaq, AjBʼit! Look upon us! Kojawilaʼ! Listen to us! Kojata!

Do not forget us! Mojatzaqo! Donʼt abandon us!

Do not discard us! Mojapiskʼalij! Do not leave us! Oh You Divine Deity in the Sky;

Oh Deified Divinity upon the Earth!

At Kabʼawil chi Kaj, chi Ulew!

Heart of the Sky, Heart of the Earth; Ukʼux Kaj, Ukʼux Ulew Gift us Our Way, Gift us Our Word; Chayaʼ taj qetal, qatzijel Give us Our Tradition, Give us Our Truth; Chayaʼ taj qetal, qatzijel Give us Our Track, Give us Our Trace; Chayaʼ taj qetal, qatzijel Give us Our Daughters, Give us Our Sons; Chayaʼ taj qetal, qatzijel Give us Our Replacements; Give us Our Descendants;

These Primordial Mountains are where the Principal Lineage of the Kʼicheʼ live out their primordial ancestral lives on the Original Mountain Places they found as those known as Our First Four Mother-Fathers; QaNabʼe Chuch-Qajaw of the Kʼicheʼ Maya Kingdom with its Primordial Lineages and their Primordial Mountain Places: Chi Pixabʼ; Jaqawitz; Chui Juyubʼ; Chi Ismachi; Qʼumaraq Aaj; Santa Cruz del Quicheʼ… And on and on it will go with the changing and transforming of the Mountain Places; Pa Juyubʼal of the ʼMountain Tribesʼ and the Noble Lords of the Great House coming to live on these Primordial Mountains of the Original Primordial Mountain People who lived on these ʼLivable Mountainsʼ before the arrival of the Kʼicheʼ Warlords and Captains who carry-out their Conquest and Settlement of the Mountain People and their Mountain Places.

All of which comes to its inevitable end with the arrival of the Castilian Captains and Warlords guided and protected by a New Sun known as Tonatiuh or Donadiu the Castilian Conquistador el Adelantado Don Pedro de Alvarado who like the Great Star Venus bears the New Age of the New Sun of the New Lords of the Land known as Kaxlan Winaq; Castilian People.

Letʼs go, letʼs get going! We must find these Kabʼawil we seek in our quest to find the One who will guide and protect us by speaking to us on our way back to our Mountains from our place here in Tollan Tzuy-Wa. Let us then find to One who will accompany us to the Forest Realm of the Many Trees of Our Highland Forest of We, the First Four Noble Lords of the Kʼicheʼ. They heard word of Citadel and this is where they went. The name of that Citadel; Tinamit was Juyubʼ Waʼ which was known as Mountain of Sustenance that was indeed spoken of as Sustenance Mountain and this is where they went; those known and named:

Bʼalam Kiʼtzeʼ;

Bʼalam Aqʼabʼ;

Majukʼutaj;

Ikʼi Bʼalam This is the name of the Citadel-Tinamit-Ciudadela they went to, in order to arrive at the splendid city sown and seated at the very center of the great empire of Quetzalcoatl: Tulan.

Upon hearing this they who were the Kabʼawil were made content; their hearts grew larger; their hearts grew strong those Kabʼawil that were named Tojil, Awilix, Jaqawitz. They were made content by what the Messenger of Xibʼalbʼa said to them; when he said what he said to them, the Kabʼawil: Tojil, Awilix, Jaqawitz… Just like that the Messenger of Xibʼalbʼa disappeared before them; they watched him disappear without seeing where he went, he just disappeared.

Messenger of Xibʼalbʼa He was there and then he wasnʼt; the Messenger, the Worker, he of Xibʼalbʼa disappeared into thin air. Now he is; now he isnʼt; then he was; then he wasnʼt. They saw not where he was or where he wasnʼt; for they knew not where he went or where was because what they saw was that he simply wasnʼt; they just knew he no longer was there where he was, because where he was, was where he now just wasnʼt; this One from Xibʼalbʼa simply disappeared; to appear again where he wasnʼt.

It was then that the Tribes arrived again before the Kabʼawil. They were just being finished off by the bitter cold drafts of freezing cold air that dropped from the icy sky, thick with hail-saqboch, as the earth became drenched with dense black rain-qʼeqʼal jabʼ that turned to ice as it froze covering the ground with white frost: saqʼbʼoqom.

Intolerable was the unbearable icy cold that they bundled themselves up against; the Tribes; Amaqʼ, one and all, could no longer tolerate the freezing cold that left them crawling about on all fours like animals to arrive there before those were the First Four Fathers:

Bʼalam Kiʼtzeʼ;

Bʼalam Aqʼabʼ;

Majukʼutaj;

Ikʼi Bʼalam Immense was the affliction of these cursed souls because of the insufferable cold. They covered their mouths; they covered their faces as they came to plead their desperate situation before the Noble Lords who they begged and beseeched to show them mercy by doing them a favor; those who were:

Bʼalam Kiʼtzeʼ;

Bʼalam Aqʼabʼ;

To keep this Sacred Narrative straight requires rites of passage that are rites of initiation to open the way for those called to ʼenter the word, enter the wayʼ of the Saq Bʼey; Sacred Path; Precious Road; Perfect Path of the Divine Ancestors that leads to being and becoming Day- keepers; AjQʼijabʼ Divinatory Priests and Priestesses who guided and protect their People of their Pueblo through the connection and communication they maintain through ʼjust sacrifice, just ancestral magic; xa pus, xa nawalʼ to form and shape the sacred relationship of sacrifice and petition to obtain what is needed to survive in live through the power and authority of the rule and command of these very same Deities; these very same Kabʼawil named here. It was just the nawal genius of the Tojiles that brought about the great reversal to bring about the disgrace and degradation of the Lords of the Pueblo. It was all because of their deep need to destroy the Tojiles that the Pueblos did what they did. Now they would need to contemplate what it was that they would do next. And so it was that they gathered together to celebrate council once again to think upon what it would take to finish off the Tojiles there on the Mountain of the Mountain People of the Mountain Pueblos.

Pueblo of Warriors;

Pueblo of Killers;

Pueblo of Assassins: Amaqʼ Kamisanel This then was when the entire Pueblo took council together; to think it through; xenaʼojin to find what it was they needed to do. “How much longer must we put up with this? Truly, their nature, their condition, their way of being is now quite powerful; they have become a powerful Pueblo of powerful People. So be it! Ta chuxoq!”

So this is what they said then when they gathered together their thoughts, kinaʼoj; when they brought forth their ideas, kinaʼoj; their genius, kinaʼoj; their imagination, kinaʼoj; their knowing of knowledge, kinaʼoj.

“We should just invade them there where they live and then kill them all! Let us arm ourselves with our arrows and our shields; chi chʼabʼ, chi pokobʼ to then climb up to where they are and finish them off, one and all! Are we not many; are we not more than they?”

This was the nawal soul and essential nature of Bʼalam Kiʼtzeʼ that was left behind as his ʼsign-retal-symbolʼ for his descendants to remember him by. Bʼalam Kiʼtzeʼ was he who left the heart, the kernel, the seed, the soul of his nawal nature of magical transformation as the divine essence of his transformative character; his transformative nature of his nawal soul this is his ancestral soul which he had left behind as what would always be remembered as his sign-retal the sign of remembrance of his nawal spirit of his ancestral soul: “May this which I leave with you serve as a remembrance of me; may this that I leave with you enable you to form-prayers, to shape-petitions; to make- prayers, to perform-petitions to me and before me your Grandfather, Father-Mam, Qajaw. This is your power-qʼaqʼal-this is your strength; this your fire; this your ember; this is your spark; this is your flame to ignite the light of your radiance,” he said when he left his sign-retal-of remembrance, his transformative essence- ukʼojeʼik-his divine nature of his Nawal Soul: Pisom Qʼaqʼal-Sacred Bundle of Burning Embers; Sacred Bundle of Ancestral Power; Ancestral Fire for the Incantation and Invocation of Divine Remembrance; Prayer and Petition of Ancestral Power: Sacred Bundle of Ancestral Fire Sacred Bundle of Ancestral Soul;

Sacred Bundle of Ancestral Seed;

Sacred Bundle of Ancestral Star;

Sacred Bundle of Ancestral Spirit;

Sacred Bundle of Ancestral Essence of Nawal Perfection;

Sacred Bundle of Ancestral Essence of Ancestral Power:

Pisom Qʼaqʼal Bʼalam Kiʼtzeʼ.”

