Lord of the Five Days: Jobʼ Qʼij This then is the Noble Kʼicheʼ Lords pleading their plea; making their petition through this prayer performed before their Kabʼawil in order to make their appeal from their ancestral heart to request the sacred sustenance of their People while clearing their road of all possible obstacles of every snare, trap, lust, longing, deception, and desire that will make them fail, falter, to then fall into the depravations placed on oneʼs path by the deceptive play that is the way of the trickster deity mentioned here; the deceiver; the mocker of men; the deceiver of women who one must stay clear for he-she is the one who causes fear that is prayed to be free of here. This then is the petitioning before the divine countenance of the Kʼicheʼ Kabʼawil; the calling out, the crying out to beg, to beseech; to request, to require; to implore, to entreat; to plead, to pray, to petition; to solicit, to supplicate; to ask for in order to make oneʼs final appeal heard by the Lord of the Five Days known to us here as Quetzalcoatl; the Great Star known s the Morning Star; the Evening Star in his Fiver Forms and Phases known by Four Names of the Five Days of the Year-Bearers; known as the Mam; the Ancient Old Ancestor; Grandfather who takes the form of the transforming image of the changing and transforming Maize Deity with all the many forms that take the many shapes of Sacred Sustenance.

These Five Divine Days also refer to the Five Days of the Year-Bearer and perhaps it was on these Five Days that the Five Sections of the Popol Wuj were played out; one Story of Origins played out on each Night of each of the Days; played out like Twins to reveal the Twinship of the Sun and Venus; Moon and the Sun; Venus and the Moon. This then is the prayer of invocation of the Story of Forming; the Story of Shaping told on each Night of these Days known as the Wayebʼ. The Wayebʼ embody this Five Day period of the changing and transforming ʼNawal Wayʼ of these Five Divine Days.

